      "Time is invisible. When your brain is overloaded, invisible time might as well not exist. '10 minutes' means nothing if you can't see it shrinking. You can't feel it passing. Until suddenly it's gone and you're late again.",
      "Visual timers solve this by making time physical. A Time Timer (the red disk that shrinks), a sand timer, or a phone timer with a visual countdown — these translate abstract time into something your brain can perceive: a shrinking shape, falling sand, a moving bar.",
      "For both you and your children, visual timers reduce transition difficulty (you can SEE the end approaching), improve time estimation (you learn what 10 minutes looks like), reduce anxiety (no surprise endings), and support task completion (the visual creates gentle urgency without panic).",
      "Use them for: morning routines (15-minute timer visible to everyone), transitions ('when the timer ends, we leave'), focused work blocks (25-minute sessions), and self-care ('10 minutes of rest — watch the timer so you don't feel guilty about stopping').",
