    readTime: "2 min",
    body: [
      "The thought arrives: 'I need to buy milk.' 'I should call the dentist.' 'That form is due Friday.' You think: 'I'll remember.' You won't. Not because you're careless — because under these conditions, your working memory has a 30-second shelf life for non-urgent information.",
      "Every thought you don't capture is a thought you'll lose. And every lost thought becomes: a missed appointment, a forgotten birthday, a child without a costume on costume day, a bill paid late. The consequences accumulate. The shame accumulates. And the solution was always the same: write it down. Immediately. Every time.",
      "This isn't about being organised or having a perfect system. It's about accepting a reality: your brain, under chronic overload, cannot hold information reliably. Once you accept this without shame, writing everything down becomes as natural as breathing. It's not a crutch. It's how you function in these conditions.",
      "The tool doesn't matter. Phone notes, voice memos, scrap of paper, a text to yourself. What matters is the habit: thought arrives → capture immediately → trust the system instead of your memory. The gap between 'I'll remember' and 'I'll write it down' is the gap between chaos and function.",
    ],
