      "The two-minute rule is simple: if a task takes less than 2 minutes, do it immediately instead of adding it to your mental list. Hang up the coat. Reply to the text. Put the dish in the dishwasher. Wipe the counter. Sign the form. Done.",
      "Why this works for overloaded brains: every task you defer — no matter how small — occupies space in your working memory. It becomes another 'open loop' your brain has to track. Ten deferred 2-minute tasks = ten open loops = significant cognitive drain. Doing them immediately closes the loop and frees the space.",
      "The rule also bypasses the starting problem. You don't have to 'decide to start' a 2-minute task — you just do it in the moment you notice it. The decision cost is zero because the rule already made the decision: under 2 minutes = now. No deliberation needed.",
      "Important caveat: this only works for tasks that genuinely take under 2 minutes. If you start 'quickly' organising a drawer and 45 minutes disappear — that wasn't a 2-minute task. Be honest about what's quick and what's a trap disguised as a small task.",
